Make the scheduler's per-round system-cycle cap env-tunable (LSW2_ITERCAP).

kIterCap is the number of SYSTEM cycles the scheduler runs before the ARM9 and
ARM7 rendezvous - the interleave granularity, and the grid on which an IRQ
becomes deliverable. The melonDS-faithful value is 64 and stays the default.

This makes it readable from LSW2_ITERCAP (clamped 8..4096) so the ARM9<->ARM7
alignment can be shifted deliberately. That alignment decides the joiner's
WM-enable/browse race: WM_Enable sets the allow flag and a MeasureChannel
completion callback clears it, and the browse's WM_GetAllowedChannel races the
two within a frame. At the default 64 the browse loses (WM error 3); at 128 the
grid shifts so the flag is still set when the browse reads it, and the joiner
associates for real (SCANNING -> DATASHARING). With that, both consoles reach
the cartridge's own state 41 and then the arena (state 42) together - the
match-start handshake completes.

The lambda reads getenv once at static-init, before any guest code runs, so the
hot path still only loads a const. Out-of-range or unset falls back to 64, so a
build with no env set is byte-for-byte the melonDS-faithful timeline.

--- a/runner/src/scheduler.cpp	2026-09-05 14:40:11.000000000 -0700
+++ b/runner/src/scheduler.cpp	2026-09-21 21:00:00.000000000 -0700
@@ -53,7 +53,18 @@
 uint64_t           g_sys_timestamp = 0;
 constexpr int      kArm9ClockShift = 1;    // ARM9 runs at 2x the system clock
-constexpr uint64_t kIterCap        = 64;   // system cycles per outer iteration
+// system cycles per outer iteration. Env-tunable (LSW2_ITERCAP, default 64 = the
+// melonDS-faithful cap) to probe the ARM9<->ARM7 interleave alignment that decides the
+// joiner WM-enable/browse race: a different cap shifts the IRQ-delivery grid
+// and the CPU rendezvous, potentially reproducing a known-good alignment.
+const uint64_t kIterCap = [] {
+    if (const char* e = std::getenv("LSW2_ITERCAP")) {
+        char* end = nullptr;
+        const unsigned long v = std::strtoul(e, &end, 0);
+        if (v >= 8u && v <= 4096u) return static_cast<uint64_t>(v);
+    }
+    return static_cast<uint64_t>(64);
+}();
